{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"El Sueño del Mara'akame ("Mara'akame's Dream") is a 2016 Mexican drama film, directed and written by Federico Cecchetti.","title":"El Sueño del Mara'akame"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film plot is about a young boy who dreams to travel to Mexico City to play with his band, but his father, a Mara'akame ("chaman hichol"), has a different plan for his future.","title":"El Sueño del Mara'akame"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film premiered at the 14th Morelia International Film Festival and was awarded Best First of Second Mexican Feature Film.","title":"El Sueño del Mara'akame"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Huichol or Wixáritari ( /wiˈraɾitaɾi/) are Native Americans, living in the Sierra Madre Occidental range in the Mexican states of Nayarit, Jalisco, Zacatecas, and Durango.","title":"Huichol people"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They are best known to the larger world as the "Huichol", however, they refer to themselves as "Wixáritari" ("the people") in their native Huichol language.","title":"Huichol people"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The adjectival form of "Wixáritari" and name for their own language is "Wixárika".","title":"Huichol people"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariana Treviño is a Mexican stage and screen actress from Monterrey.","title":"Mariana Treviño"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She became known to a wider domestic audience through her role as Lupita in the popular jukebox musical "Mentiras", which she played in over a thousand performances.","title":"Mariana Treviño"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Following the success of Mentiras, Treviño went on to appear in Mexican comedy films such as "No sé si cortarme las venas o dejármelas largas" (2013) and "Amor de Mis Amores" (2014).","title":"Mariana Treviño"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" As of 2015, she co-stars with Luis Gerardo Méndez in Netflix' first ever Spanish-language original production "Club de Cuervos".","title":"Mariana Treviño"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Treviño received two nominations at the 59th Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actress for "La Vida Inmoral de la Pareja Ideal", and Best Actress in a Minor Role for "El Sueño del Mara'akame".","title":"Mariana Treviño"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Huichol people of western Mexico exude an enduring spirit and passion to hold on to their traditions.","title":"Huichol Route to Huiricuta"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This is exemplified in the pilgrimage route between Nayarit and Huiricuta, stretching nearly 800 km, wherein dozens of sacred sites are visited along the way.","title":"Huichol Route to Huiricuta"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The route follows the old pre-Hispanic trade routes between the Gulf of Mexico and the Pacific Ocean.","title":"Huichol Route to Huiricuta"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" One of the most important of these routes, to Huiricuta or Wirikuta, is known as such for its historical importance in the preservation of Huichol cultural as well as the sheer proliferation of pilgrims that follow the route.","title":"Huichol Route to Huiricuta"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Because the Huichol have no written language, the pilgrimage has the added importance of linking populations of Huichol and disseminating knowledge of culture and reinforcing cultural practices.","title":"Huichol Route to Huiricuta"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Huichol art broadly groups the most traditional and most recent innovations in the folk art and handcrafts produced by the Huichol people, who live in the states of Jalisco, Durango, Zacatecas and Nayarit in Mexico.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The unifying factor of the work is the colorful decoration using symbols and designs which date back centuries.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The most common and commercially successful products are “yarn paintings” and objects decorated with small commercially produced beads.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Yarn paintings consist of commercial yarn pressed into boards coated with wax and resin and are derived from a ceremonial tablet called a neirika.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Huichol have a long history of beading, making the beads from clay, shells, corals, seeds and more and using them to make jewelry and to decorate bowls and other items.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The “modern” beadwork usually consists of masks and wood sculptures covered in small, brightly colored commercial beads fastened with wax and resin.","title":"Huichol art"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Zapata: El sueño del héroe is the Soundtrack for the Mexican film "" and was released in May 2004, to accompany the release of the film.","title":"Zapata: El sueño del héroe (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The lead single was the song ""Lucharé por tu amor"" by Alejandro Fernández and reached the top 10 of the Billboard Latin Pop Airplay chart.","title":"Zapata: El sueño del héroe (soundtrack)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: The film’s plot features a Mara'akame, a Huichol shaman, linking the movie to the Huichol people.
