d creating radical political spaces in black communities across the United States.","title":"African-American bookstores"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" By the 1990s, African-American bookstores earned significant attention from more politically moderate and business oriented media outlets such as the magazine Black Enterprise.","title":"African-American bookstores"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" In the 2000s and 2010s, however, as independent bookstores of all kinds declined and bookstores chains and Amazon increasingly sold black-authored books, the number of African-American bookstores declined rapidly, dropping from over 250 to just over 70.","title":"African-American bookstores"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Rose Catherine Pinkney (born 1964) is an American television development executive.","title":"Rose Catherine Pinkney"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She was hired as the VP Development and Original Programming for TV Land in 2012.","title":"Rose Catherine Pinkney"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Pinkney has previously served as Director of Programming at Twentieth Century Fox Television, Senior Vice President of Comedy Development at Paramount Pictures Television and executive vice president of programming and production at TV One.","title":"Rose Catherine Pinkney"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" She most recently served as the head of the television arm of Laurence Fishburne's production company, Cinema Gypsy.","title":"Rose Catherine Pinkney"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Among Pinkney's accolades are Network Journal's 25 Most Influential Black Women in Business, Cable World's Top 50 Women in Cable and Black Enterprise's Top 50 Entertainment Executives.","title":"Rose Catherine Pinkney"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Our World with Black Enterprise is a weekly television show currently hosted by Paul Brunson, and, during its first season, Marc Lamont Hill which is produced by Black Enterprise, Inc. It airs on Saturday and Sunday across a range of local television stations and focuses on v