:" He became proficient on the piano and violin, and could play most band instruments.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1882 he had the opportunity to travel with a noted English orchestra through the United States.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The cornetist that had come with the orchestra became ill and was sent back to England.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Heed was highly recommended and was engaged to fill his the cornetist's place.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" He received encomiums from the press and public in every city and town visited.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" A year later, in 1883, Heed accepted an engagement to become the leader of the Providence Brigade Band.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" This was a position that he held until he was called back to New Jersey to conduct another orchestra and band.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Soon thereafter, he went to Worcester, Massachusetts and spent eight years as a teacher of bands.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" His next position was a cornetist for Voss's First Regiment Band in Newark, New Jersey.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":12,"text":" It was after the Metronome article was written that Mr. Heed went with John Phillip Sousa's band as a soloist and arranger before contracting tuberculosis in the 1890s and dying in Newark, New Jersey on February 12, 1908.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":13,"text":" He died leaving no children.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":14,"text":" He was buried near his family in Union Cemetery in Hackettstown, New Jersey.","title":"John Clifford Heed"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Peter Jacob (born in Kerala, India 1985) was a 2016 Democratic Party candidate in New Jersey's 7th congressional district who ran against Leonard Lanc